# Deny everything, then name what the build needs.
#
# The allow-list direction is the point: with a deny-list, a directory added
# later enters the image by default, and the two that would do real damage are
# already in this repository. `configs/` holds the example inventory and the
# example rate card — placeholder routes and invented prices — and an image
# carrying them is one that can be pointed at them in production by a single
# environment variable. `tools/contract/` pulls a node_modules tree that has no
# business in a Go runtime image.
#
# This file is also the Dockerfile's `COPY . .` manifest, so the set of paths
# that reach the builder is decided here and in no second place.
*

!go.mod
# Absent today: Pensara's module requires nothing outside the standard library.
# Named so that the first dependency does not also become a build failure.
!go.sum
!cmd/**
!internal/**

# The publisher's attribution table, and ONLY it. Named as one file rather than
# as `configs/**` on purpose: the hazard this file's header describes is the
# example inventory and the example rate card — placeholder routes and invented
# prices that a single environment variable could point a production process at.
# The attribution table is neither. It is a public statement of who released
# which weights, it carries no route, no address, no price and no credential,
# and the publisher cannot start without it. A second file added to `configs/`
# still does not enter the image.
!configs/model-attribution.json

# Test files and the fixtures they write are not compiled into the binary, and
# `go build` would not read them, but they are context bytes uploaded to the
# builder on every build.
**/*_test.go
internal/contract/testdata/**
